"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\">\n<div class=\"tabs-container\" id=\"95f8142e-70a0-4d7c-b515-54560315df83\">\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<button class=\"tab-button active\" data-for-tab=\"HTML\">HTML</button>\n<button class=\"tab-button\" data-for-tab=\"Markdown\">Markdown</button>\n<button class=\"tab-button\" data-for-tab=\"Hide\">Hide</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"tab-content active\" data-tab=\"HTML\">\n<h1>Tutorial Outline</h1>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Creating structured outline...</p>\n\n</div>\n\n\n<div class=\"tab-content\" data-tab=\"Markdown\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-markdown\"># Tutorial Outline\n\n**Status:** Creating structured outline...\n\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n\n<div class=\"tab-content\" data-tab=\"Hide\">\n\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\">\n<div class=\"tabs-container\" id=\"5826f564-4888-4b1e-817a-d9ab6ec4026b\">\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<button class=\"tab-button active\" data-for-tab=\"HTML\">HTML</button>\n<button class=\"tab-button\" data-for-tab=\"Markdown\">Markdown</button>\n<button class=\"tab-button\" data-for-tab=\"Hide\">Hide</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"tab-content active\" data-tab=\"HTML\">\n<h2>Building Your First Kotlin Application with Gradle in IntelliJ IDEA</h2>\n<p>This tutorial guides beginners through the process of setting up a professional development environment using IntelliJ IDEA and the Gradle build system. You will learn how to initialize a project, understand the Kotlin project structure, and write a &quot;Hello World&quot; program that runs in the console.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>What You'll Learn</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Configure a new Kotlin/JVM project using the Gradle build system.</li>\n<li>Navigate the standard Kotlin project directory structure.</li>\n<li>Identify the role of the build.gradle.kts file.</li>\n<li>Write and execute a standard Kotlin main function.</li>\n<li>Utilize IntelliJ IDEA’s built-in tools to compile and run code.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Prerequisites</h3>\n<h4>Required</h4>\n<p><strong>IntelliJ IDEA</strong> (software)</p>\n<p>Community or Ultimate edition (Version 2023.1 or newer recommended).</p>\n<p>Download: https://www.jetbrains.com/idea/download/</p>\n<p><strong>Java Development Kit (JDK)</strong> (software)</p>\n<p>Version 11, 17, or 21 (Can be downloaded directly through IntelliJ during the tutorial).</p>\n<p>Download: https://adoptium.net/</p>\n<p><strong>Basic Computer Familiarity</strong> (knowledge)</p>\n<p>Basic familiarity with using a computer (navigating folders and typing). No prior programming experience is required.</p>\n<p>Download:</p>\n<h4>Optional</h4>\n<p><strong>Hardware Requirements</strong> (hardware)</p>\n<p>A computer running Windows, macOS, or Linux with at least 8GB of RAM recommended for smooth IDE performance.</p>\n<p>Download:</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Tutorial Steps</h3>\n<h4>Step 1: Initialize a New Kotlin Gradle Project</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To use the IntelliJ Project Wizard to generate the necessary scaffolding for a Gradle-based Kotlin application.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Open IntelliJ IDEA and select 'New Project.'</li>\n<li>Name the project (e.g., 'KotlinHelloWorld').</li>\n<li>Select Kotlin as the language and Gradle as the build system.</li>\n<li>Choose Kotlin for the Gradle DSL (Build script language).</li>\n<li>Select a JDK (use the 'Download JDK' option if none are present).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Screenshot needed</em></p>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> A new IDE window opens with a generated project structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Time:</strong> ~7 min</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Step 2: Explore the Project Structure and Gradle Configuration</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To understand where source code lives and how Gradle manages the project.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Locate the src/main/kotlin folder in the Project tool window.</li>\n<li>Open the build.gradle.kts file in the root directory.</li>\n<li>Identify the kotlin('jvm') plugin and the dependencies block.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Includes code/commands</em></p>\n<p><em>Screenshot needed</em></p>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> The user understands that source code goes in src/main/kotlin and settings go in build.gradle.kts.</p>\n<p><strong>Time:</strong> ~5 min</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Step 3: Create the Main Kotlin File</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To create the specific file where the application logic will reside.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right-click on the src/main/kotlin directory.</li>\n<li>Select New &gt; Kotlin Class/File.</li>\n<li>Type Main and ensure 'File' is selected from the list.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Screenshot needed</em></p>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> A new, empty file named Main.kt appears in the project tree and opens in the editor.</p>\n<p><strong>Time:</strong> ~3 min</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Step 4: Write the 'Hello World' Code</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To implement the entry point of the application using Kotlin syntax.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Define the main function: fun main() { ... }</li>\n<li>Add the print statement: println('Hello, World!')</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Includes code/commands</em></p>\n<p><em>Screenshot needed</em></p>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> A syntactically correct Kotlin file ready for execution.</p>\n<p><strong>Time:</strong> ~5 min</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Step 5: Build and Run the Application</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To compile the Kotlin code into bytecode and execute it using the Gradle runner.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Locate the green 'Play' arrow in the gutter next to the fun main() line.</li>\n<li>Click the arrow and select Run 'MainKt'.</li>\n<li>Observe the 'Run' tool window at the bottom of the IDE.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Screenshot needed</em></p>\n<p><strong>Expected Outcome:</strong> The project builds successfully, and the text 'Hello, World!' is printed to the console.</p>\n<p><strong>Time:</strong> ~10 min</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> ✅ Complete</p>\n\n</div>\n\n\n<div class=\"tab-content\" data-tab=\"Markdown\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-markdown\">## Building Your First Kotlin Application with Gradle in IntelliJ IDEA\n\nThis tutorial guides beginners through the process of setting up a professional development environment using IntelliJ IDEA and the Gradle build system. You will learn how to initialize a project, understand the Kotlin project structure, and write a \"Hello World\" program that runs in the console.\n\n**Estimated Time:** 30 minutes\n\n---\n\n### What You'll Learn\n- Configure a new Kotlin/JVM project using the Gradle build system.\n- Navigate the standard Kotlin project directory structure.\n- Identify the role of the build.gradle.kts file.\n- Write and execute a standard Kotlin main function.\n- Utilize IntelliJ IDEA’s built-in tools to compile and run code.\n\n---\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n#### Required\n**IntelliJ IDEA** (software)\n\nCommunity or Ultimate edition (Version 2023.1 or newer recommended).\n\nDownload: https://www.jetbrains.com/idea/download/\n\n**Java Development Kit (JDK)** (software)\n\nVersion 11, 17, or 21 (Can be downloaded directly through IntelliJ during the tutorial).\n\nDownload: https://adoptium.net/\n\n**Basic Computer Familiarity** (knowledge)\n\nBasic familiarity with using a computer (navigating folders and typing). No prior programming experience is required.\n\nDownload: \n\n#### Optional\n**Hardware Requirements** (hardware)\n\nA computer running Windows, macOS, or Linux with at least 8GB of RAM recommended for smooth IDE performance.\n\nDownload: \n\n---\n\n### Tutorial Steps\n#### Step 1: Initialize a New Kotlin Gradle Project\n\n**Purpose:** To use the IntelliJ Project Wizard to generate the necessary scaffolding for a Gradle-based Kotlin application.\n\n**Actions:**\n- Open IntelliJ IDEA and select 'New Project.'\n- Name the project (e.g., 'KotlinHelloWorld').\n- Select Kotlin as the language and Gradle as the build system.\n- Choose Kotlin for the Gradle DSL (Build script language).\n- Select a JDK (use the 'Download JDK' option if none are present).\n\n*Screenshot needed*\n\n**Expected Outcome:** A new IDE window opens with a generated project structure.\n\n**Time:** ~7 min\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Explore the Project Structure and Gradle Configuration\n\n**Purpose:** To understand where source code lives and how Gradle manages the project.\n\n**Actions:**\n- Locate the src/main/kotlin folder in the Project tool window.\n- Open the build.gradle.kts file in the root directory.\n- Identify the kotlin('jvm') plugin and the dependencies block.\n\n*Includes code/commands*\n\n*Screenshot needed*\n\n**Expected Outcome:** The user understands that source code goes in src/main/kotlin and settings go in build.gradle.kts.\n\n**Time:** ~5 min\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Create the Main Kotlin File\n\n**Purpose:** To create the specific file where the application logic will reside.\n\n**Actions:**\n- Right-click on the src/main/kotlin directory.\n- Select New &gt; Kotlin Class/File.\n- Type Main and ensure 'File' is selected from the list.\n\n*Screenshot needed*\n\n**Expected Outcome:** A new, empty file named Main.kt appears in the project tree and opens in the editor.\n\n**Time:** ~3 min\n\n---\n\n#### Step 4: Write the 'Hello World' Code\n\n**Purpose:** To implement the entry point of the application using Kotlin syntax.\n\n**Actions:**\n- Define the main function: fun main() { ... }\n- Add the print statement: println('Hello, World!')\n\n*Includes code/commands*\n\n*Screenshot needed*\n\n**Expected Outcome:** A syntactically correct Kotlin file ready for execution.\n\n**Time:** ~5 min\n\n---\n\n#### Step 5: Build and Run the Application\n\n**Purpose:** To compile the Kotlin code into bytecode and execute it using the Gradle runner.\n\n**Actions:**\n- Locate the green 'Play' arrow in the gutter next to the fun main() line.\n- Click the arrow and select Run 'MainKt'.\n- Observe the 'Run' tool window at the bottom of the IDE.\n\n*Screenshot needed*\n\n**Expected Outcome:** The project builds successfully, and the text 'Hello, World!' is printed to the console.\n\n**Time:** ~10 min\n\n---\n\n**Status:** ✅ Complete\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n\n<div class=\"tab-content\" data-tab=\"Hide\">\n\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"